/**
 * Standalone (no-collection) UVA problems, batch R (7 problems), under the 4-test-case standard
 * (Sample + boundary + tricky/adversarial case + stress test near the stated upper bound).
 *
 * - uva-11659-informants: this is "maximum true count among ALL satisfying assignments" of a
 *   Horn-2SAT-like formula (every assertion is a clause with at least one negative literal), which is
 *   NOT the same problem as plain 2-SAT satisfiability. Two earlier attempts both failed against the
 *   real sample: (a) checking only whether a person's own "reliable" and "not reliable" literals reach
 *   each other in the implication graph misses PAIRWISE mutual-exclusion contradictions between two
 *   DIFFERENT people (neither self-contradicts alone, but they can't both be reliable simultaneously);
 *   (b) the standard SCC-based 2-SAT satisfiability check plus its usual topological-order variable
 *   assignment rule gives A valid satisfying assignment, but not necessarily the MAXIMUM-true one
 *   (wrong on 2 of the first 3 real sample cases when tested). Given the problem's own explicit bound
 *   i<=20 (a strong hint toward exponential-in-i search), solved via brute force over all 2^i subsets,
 *   with each person's positive/negative requirements precomputed as bitmasks so validity-checking one
 *   subset is O(i) via bitwise AND -- confirmed both correct (cross-checked against an independent
 *   brute-force verifier on the exact 3 cases that broke the two earlier approaches) and fast (the
 *   worst case i=20,a=800 completes in low tens of milliseconds). Verified against the full official
 *   sample (21 cases) after adopting this approach. Boundary: a single informant with zero assertions
 *   (trivially reliable alone, no constraints to violate). Tricky: the minimal genuine
 *   self-contradiction, a single informant who asserts himself unreliable (if he's reliable, his own
 *   statement must hold, meaning he's not reliable -- a direct one-way contradiction with no cycle
 *   needed), giving 1 (he's excluded, the other informant is unconstrained). Stress: i=20, a=800 (both
 *   stated maximums), random assertions.
 * - uva-11677-alarm-clock: straightforward minute-difference with day wraparound when the alarm time
 *   is at or before the current time. Verified against the official sample. Boundary: 23:59 to 00:00 (a
 *   1-minute wraparound across midnight). Tricky: identical current and alarm time -- since the
 *   wraparound condition uses <= (not <), this forces a FULL 24-hour (1440-minute) sleep rather than 0,
 *   reasoned directly from the problem's own framing (she sets the alarm for when she wants to wake up,
 *   and an alarm "at the current time" must mean a full day later, not an instant). Stress: 3000 random
 *   test cases in one input.
 * - uva-11678-cards-exchange: the maximum trade size is min(|Alice's distinct values not owned by
 *   Betty|, |Betty's distinct values not owned by Alice|) -- cards common to both collections are
 *   irrelevant to trade (neither girl needs them from the other), confirmed by reproducing the
 *   problem's own worked example exactly (Alice/Betty's example sets give exclusive counts of 6 and 4
 *   respectively, min=4, matching the stated answer). Verified against the official sample. Boundary:
 *   both girls own only the exact same single card value (0 exclusive cards on either side, nothing
 *   tradeable). Tricky: the statement's own worked example, used as a permanent regression test
 *   independent of the DB's scraped Sample. Stress: A=B=10000 (both stated maximums) random card
 *   values.
 * - uva-11689-soda-surpler: direct simulation of the exchange cycle (buy floor(bottles/c) new sodas,
 *   drink them all, add the resulting new empty bottles to the leftover remainder, repeat until too few
 *   bottles remain) -- hand-verified against the official sample's own first two cases by tracing the
 *   cascading exchanges by hand before trusting the loop. Boundary: zero bottles at all (0 sodas,
 *   trivially). Tricky: reuses the official sample's own first case (9 bottles, cost 3 per soda -> 4
 *   sodas via a genuine 2-round cascade: buy 3, get 3 new empties, buy 1 more) as a permanent
 *   regression test. Stress: N=14 (largest N<15) cases with values near the stated bounds.
 * - uva-11728-alternate-task: re-derived that "sum of factors" means the FULL divisor sum sigma(n)
 *   (including 1 and n itself), not just proper divisors -- confirmed against the official sample's own
 *   S=1 case (answer 1: sigma(1)=1 is the only n giving a divisor-sum of exactly 1; under a
 *   proper-divisor-sum reading, infinitely many primes would tie at proper-sum=1, making "largest"
 *   undefined, which can't be right). Search is a simple O(S*sqrt(S)) scan from S-1 down to 1 for the
 *   largest matching n (S<=1000, trivially fast). Verified against the official sample (20 cases).
 *   Boundary: S=0 (immediate terminator, 0 cases -- distinct from S=1's real answer of 1). Tricky: S=2
 *   (no n has sigma(n)=2 at all, since sigma(1)=1 and sigma(n)>=n+1>=3 for all n>=2 -- answer -1).
 *   Stress: all 1000 possible S values from 1 to 1000 in a single input.
 * - uva-11730-number-transformation: BFS over reachable integer values, where from value v you may
 *   move to v+p for each DISTINCT PRIME factor p of v (excluding v itself, so a prime v has zero valid
 *   moves at all) -- verified against the official sample, including reproducing the problem's own
 *   worked example (6->9->12 in 2 steps: +3 twice, since 3 is a prime factor of both 6 and 9) exactly.
 *   Boundary: S=T (0 transformations needed, trivially, even before considering any factor logic).
 *   Tricky: reuses the statement's own worked example (6,12 -> 2) as a permanent regression test.
 *   Stress: S=1,T=1000 (the largest possible value spread under the stated bounds), stressing the BFS
 *   over the full reachable range.
 * - uva-11743-credit-check: the Luhn algorithm exactly as specified (double every digit at an EVEN
 *   distance-from-the-right position, i.e. the 2nd, 4th, 6th... from the right, subtracting 9 from any
 *   result over 9; sum everything; valid iff the total is a multiple of 10). Verified against the
 *   official sample, including reproducing the problem statement's own fully-worked example number
 *   (5181 2710 9900 0012, hand-computed total 45, correctly Invalid) exactly. Boundary: 16 repeated 1s
 *   (hand-computed: 8 undoubled 1s + 8 doubled-to-2 digits = 8+16=24, not a multiple of 10, Invalid).
 *   Tricky: reuses the statement's own worked example directly as a permanent regression test. Stress:
 *   1000 random 16-digit card numbers in the stated 4-groups-of-4 format.
 */
